#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

=test:alfe:windows= (native Windows runner, =allow_failure: true=), job
16756197957 on master pipeline 2885678935: the suite completes with 1208 checks
and *4 failures, all in one test* —
=CAD-DRIVE-PROTOCOL-ACTIONS-THREE-EVALS-COUNTER-AWARE=, on
=(EVAL-RESULT-STATUS RESULT)=.

The same test passes on Linux (SBCL and CCL) and on macOS.

* Context that matters

The immediately preceding run of that lane (job 16755546814) was WORSE: it
aborted mid-suite inside =PROTOCOL-MOCK-CAD-FULL-LIFECYCLE= with an unhandled
condition and reported nothing at all. So the lane was already red before
2026-09-26's work; what changed is that it now runs to completion and isolates
the failure to one test, which is what makes this ticket possible.

* Why it is probably platform, not logic

The test drives the FILE protocol against the mock: three =:eval= actions, each
waiting for =DONE N= with N the next request counter. It is exactly the shape
that Windows file semantics disturb, and the repository already carries two
findings of that class:

- =write-atomic-file='s overwriting rename is NOT atomic on Windows (UIOP does
  delete-then-rename), so a reader can transiently see the target missing --
  see =write-atomic-file-concurrent-rename-windows= (closed) and the Windows
  skip in =protocol-write-atomic-file-contention=.
- Readers must use =:if-does-not-exist nil= and never probe-then-open, because
  the delete gap of a hot-rewritten file makes TOCTOU fire.

So the first hypothesis is that the mock's =status.txt= / =stdout.txt= updates
are read across such a gap, and =eval-result-status= comes back NIL (or stale)
for one of the three actions rather than =:ok=.

* Directions

1. Get the 4 failing checks' actual values from the job trace (the names are
   there; the values are truncated in the summary) -- which of the three
   actions, and what status came back.
2. Decide between the two honest outcomes, and do not fudge the difference:
   - the ASSERTION is platform-dependent (a status legitimately observable as
     pending on Windows) -> relax it for Windows the way the contention test
     does, with the reason at the call site; or
   - the PROTOCOL DRIVER has a real race that Windows merely exposes -> fix
     the driver, since a real CAD on that platform would hit it too. This is
     the outcome that matters, and it is the reason this is not simply skipped.
3. Only then consider =:skip-on-os=; a skip chosen before step 1 would hide
   the second outcome.

* Diagnosed and fixed — clautolisp 2.2.122

Step 1 of /Directions/ answered the question: the failing check is
=(EVAL-RESULT-STATUS RESULT)= evaluating to *=:ABORTED=*, not to a stale
=:SUCCESS=. =:ABORTED= is set in exactly one place
(=backend-cad-common.lisp=, =wait-done=): =wait-for-status-prefix= did not
match within the request timeout. So it is a GIVE-UP, not the
stale-status race the ticket's first hypothesis proposed -- and the fault is in
the TEST MOCK, not the driver.

=SPAWN-MOCK-CAD-RUNTIME= was a =DOTIMES= over =cycles=, and each round waited
for =stdin.txt= for at most *two seconds*, then published =RUNNING n= / =DONE n=
*anyway* and moved on. Two consequences:

1. a slow round CONSUMED the budget, so with =:cycles 3= and three actions the
   third request was never acknowledged and the driver timed out -- which is the
   =:ABORTED= the Windows lane reported;
2. a =DONE= published for a request that never arrived is a status the driver
   could match against a send it has not yet made -- latent nondeterminism on
   every platform, not only Windows.

The mock now SERVES =cycles= requests instead of SPENDING =cycles= rounds: the
counter follows the requests actually served, so it cannot run ahead of the
driver, and the only cap is one overall deadline (20 s) whose job is to stop a
mock nobody feeds -- its test then fails on its own assertion instead of hanging.
Every one of the six call sites sends exactly =:cycles= actions, so this is what
they all meant.

*Reproduced on Linux before fixing*, which is what the ticket asked for rather
than a platform-specific guess: =cad-mock-serves-a-request-that-arrives-late=
sends its action 3 seconds after READY -- past the old per-round budget -- and
against the old mock it fails with

: a late request must still be acknowledged; got :ABORTED

the same symptom, the same word, on a machine with a fast filesystem. With the
fix it passes, and the echo assertion proves the request was really consumed
rather than answered by a status the mock had run ahead to publish.

alfe suite 1232 checks, 0 failures on SBCL.

* Acceptance criteria

- [X] *A test that fails on Linux too, so the fix is not platform-folklore* --
      =cad-mock-serves-a-request-that-arrives-late=, red with the old mock and
      with the same =:ABORTED=.
- [X] *Nothing was skipped.* No =:skip-on-os=, no relaxed assertion: the mock's
      contract was wrong and is now right on every platform.
- [ ] =test:alfe:windows= green on the runner. The symptom it reported is now
      impossible for the reason diagnosed above; whether it was the ONLY reason
      is what that lane will say. It is =allow_failure: true=, so it gates
      nothing meanwhile.

* Note

Filed per AGENTS.md: a defect noticed while doing something else gets its own
ticket rather than a mention. Noticed while reading the Windows results for
=clautolisp-distributed-native-libraries-not-loaded=.
